Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>1  Planning an application</H1>
<HR>
In this chapter you will plan the address book, write the part of it that remembers the contacts, test that
part with a program of its own, and open a first window listing the contacts. Along the way you'll meet
<I>classes</I>, JavaScript's way of making many objects of the same kind, and <I>Maps</I>, a better kind of
object for looking things up.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Planning an application</H2>
Snake, at the end of the first book, was small enough to plan in your head. An application isn't: before long
it has a dozen windows, menus and dialogue boxes, and it's easy to lose track of what goes where. So start by
writing down two things.<P>
<B>What it remembers.</B> An address book remembers contacts. For each one:<P>
<PRE>
title, first name, surname, company, job title,
address, postcode,
work, home and mobile telephone numbers,
email address, web address, birthday,
category (Family, Friends, Work or Other), notes
</PRE>
<B>What happens.</B> The things a person does with it, in their words:<P>
<PRE>
see the contacts in a list, sorted by name
choose one or more of them
add a contact; change one; delete some
find the contacts that match a word
sort by surname, first name or company
keep address books in files, and open several at once
swap contacts with other programs
undo a mistake
</PRE>
Those lists are the plan for Part One: each chapter makes one or two lines of it work.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The model and the view</H2>
Look at the two lists again. The first is about <I>information</I>; the second is mostly about
<I>windows</I> - lists, choosing, menus, files. The most useful decision you can make about an application is
to keep those two apart:<P>
<DL>
<DT>The model
<DD>holds the information and the rules about it: the contacts, how they're sorted, what makes a contact
valid. It knows nothing about windows, menus or the mouse.<P>
<DT>The view
<DD>shows the model on the screen and lets the person change it. It asks the model for what to show, and asks
the model to make changes.<P>
</DL>
Keeping them apart pays off again and again. The model can be tested without opening a window (you'll do that
later in this chapter). Several windows can show the same model at once. And the model can be used in another
program: in Part Two, !Organiser's address section uses !Contacts' model without a single change.<P>
In !Contacts the model is a module called <B>Model</B>, and the first view is <B>MainWindow</B>.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>The application directory</H2>
!Contacts1 is an application directory like !Snake in the first book:<P>
<DL>
<DT>!Boot, !Run, !Sprites, !Help
<DD>as in !Snake. !Run sets <CODE>Contacts1$Dir</CODE> to the directory, loads the icons and runs
!RunImage. (Each stage in this book has its own name - <CODE>Contacts2$Dir</CODE> and so on - so they don't
get in each other's way.)<P>
<DT>!RunImage
<DD>where the program starts.<P>
<DT>Model, MainWindow
<DD>the model and the window.<P>
<DT>Sample
<DD>ten contacts to start with. (Until chapter 7 the address book isn't kept in a file, so the program fills
it with these each time it starts.)<P>
<DT>Check
<DD>a program that tests the model.<P>
<DT>Toolkit
<DD>the toolkit modules this stage uses: for now, just <B>Emitter</B>.<P>
</DL>
!Run is the same as !Snake's, apart from the names:<P>
<PRE>
| !Run file for !Contacts1
Set Contacts1$Dir &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;
IconSprites &lt;Contacts1$Dir&gt;.!Sprites
Run &lt;Contacts1$Dir&gt;.!RunImage %*0
</PRE>
!RunImage is short, because the work is done in the other modules:<P>
<PRE>
// Contacts - an address book for RISC OS.
// !RunImage is where it starts: it makes an address book (the Book
// in ./Model), fills it with the contacts in ./Sample, and opens a
// window showing them (./MainWindow).
import { Book } from './Model';
import { CONTACTS } from './Sample';
import { MainWindow } from './MainWindow';

export default function start(task) {
  const book = new Book();
  for (const c of CONTACTS) book.add(c);
  new MainWindow(task, book);
}
</PRE>
It makes a new, empty address book, adds the sample contacts to it, and opens a window on it. Everything in
it is something you'll write in this chapter.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Classes</H2>
In the first book, when a program needed several things of the same kind - Snake's cells, Doodle's lines - it
made objects with <CODE>{ ... }</CODE> and kept them in arrays, and wrote functions to work on them. That works
well for simple things. For something with its own information <I>and</I> its own operations, such as an
address book, JavaScript has a neater way: a <I>class</I>.<P>
A class is a description of a kind of object: what information each one holds, and what it can do. Here is
the start of the Book class, lines 10 to 32 of Model:<P>
<PRE>
export class Book extends Emitter {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.contacts = new Map();         // id -&gt; contact
    this.nextId = 1;
  }

  get size() { return this.contacts.size; }
  get(id) { return this.contacts.get(id); }
  all() { return [...this.contacts.values()]; }

  add(fields) {
    const id = fields.id ?? this.nextId;
    this.nextId = Math.max(this.nextId, id + 1);
    const c = { ...fields, id };
    this.contacts.set(id, c);
    this.emit('change', { added: c });
    return c;
  }

  /** The contacts in order of surname (or company). */
  list() { return this.all().sort(compare); }
}
</PRE>
There's a lot that's new here, so take it a piece at a time.<P>
<H3>class and new</H3>
<CODE>class Book { ... }</CODE> describes what a Book is. It doesn't make one: you do that with
<CODE>new</CODE>, as !RunImage does:<P>
<PRE>
const book = new Book();
</PRE>
Each <CODE>new Book()</CODE> makes a separate address book, with its own contacts. That's why a class is
better than a module's variables for this: in chapter 7 !Contacts will open several address books at once,
and each will be a Book of its own.<P>
You've been using classes since the first book without knowing it: <CODE>new Menu(...)</CODE> and
<CODE>new Date()</CODE> make objects of the classes Menu and Date.<P>
<H3>The constructor and this</H3>
The <CODE>constructor</CODE> is a function that runs when a new Book is made, to set it up. Inside it, and
inside all the class's functions, <CODE>this</CODE> means "the Book this is happening to". So
<CODE>this.contacts = new Map()</CODE> gives the new Book a property called <CODE>contacts</CODE>, and
<CODE>this.nextId = 1</CODE> another one. Every Book has its own <CODE>contacts</CODE> and its own
<CODE>nextId</CODE>.<P>
<H3>Methods</H3>
The functions written inside a class - <CODE>get</CODE>, <CODE>all</CODE>, <CODE>add</CODE>,
<CODE>list</CODE> - are its <I>methods</I>, just like the methods of arrays and strings you used in the
first book. You call them with a dot: <CODE>book.add({ first: 'Ann' })</CODE>. Notice that you don't write
<CODE>function</CODE> in front of them, and that there are no commas between them.<P>
<H3>Getters</H3>
<CODE>get size() { ... }</CODE> is a method you use as if it were a property, without brackets:
<CODE>book.size</CODE>. It's worked out each time you ask, so it's always right. (Arrays'
<CODE>length</CODE> works the same way.)<P>
<H3>extends and super</H3>
<CODE>class Book extends Emitter</CODE> says a Book is a special kind of Emitter: it can do everything an
Emitter can (you'll see what that is shortly), and more. Emitter is the <I>parent</I> class. A class that
extends another must call <CODE>super()</CODE> at the start of its constructor, to let the parent set up its
part of the object first.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Maps</H2>
The contacts are kept in a <I>Map</I>. A Map is like an object used for looking things up: it holds pairs of a
<I>key</I> and a <I>value</I>, and finds the value for a key quickly. Here the key is each contact's
<I>id</I>, a number that's different for every contact, and the value is the contact:<P>
<PRE>
const m = new Map();
m.set(7, { first: 'Ann' });      // key 7
m.get(7)                         // { first: 'Ann' }
m.has(7)                         // true
m.delete(7);
m.size                           // how many pairs
[...m.values()]                  // all the values, as an array
</PRE>
Why not keep the contacts in an array, as the first book would have done? Because !Contacts will often need
to find one contact among many - the one being changed, the one being deleted - and an array would have to be
searched from the start every time. With a Map, <CODE>this.contacts.get(id)</CODE> finds it at once, however
many there are.<P>
Why an id, and not the contact's name? Because names change (Ann Smith marries and becomes Ann Jones), and two
people can have the same name. An id is fixed, and belongs to one contact only.<P>
<H3>Adding a contact</H3>
<CODE>add</CODE> takes an object of fields, such as <CODE>{ first: 'Ann', last: 'Smith' }</CODE>, and has
two things in it you haven't seen before:<P>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>fields.id ?? this.nextId</CODE>: <CODE>??</CODE> gives the value on its left, unless that is
<CODE>null</CODE> or <CODE>undefined</CODE>, in which case it gives the value on its right. So a contact that
already has an id keeps it, and a new one gets the next free number.<P>
<LI><CODE>{ ...fields, id }</CODE> makes a new object with all of <CODE>fields</CODE>' properties
(<CODE>...</CODE>, <I>spread</I>, copies them in) and then an <CODE>id</CODE>. Making a new object, rather
than adding the id to the one it was given, means the Book has a contact of its own that nobody else can
change behind its back.<P>
</UL>
<CODE>all()</CODE> uses spread too: <CODE>[...this.contacts.values()]</CODE> makes an array of every value
in the Map.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Sorting names</H2>
The rest of Model is two small functions for showing a contact, and the rule for sorting:<P>
<PRE>
/** The name to show for a contact: 'Smith, Ann', or its company. */
export function nameOf(c) {
  if (c.last &amp;&amp; c.first) return `${c.last}, ${c.first}`;
  return c.last || c.first || c.company || '(no name)';
}
/** The first telephone number a contact has. */
export const phoneOf = (c) =&gt; c.mobile || c.work || c.home || '';

// by surname (or company, if there isn't one), then first name
function compare(a, b) {
  const key = (c) =&gt; [c.last || c.company, c.first];
  const ka = key(a), kb = key(b);
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; ka.length; i++) {
    const d = (ka[i] ?? '').localeCompare(kb[i] ?? '', 'en',
      { sensitivity: 'base' });
    if (d) return d;
  }
  return a.id - b.id;
}
</PRE>
<CODE>nameOf</CODE> uses <CODE>||</CODE> in a way the first book didn't: <CODE>a || b</CODE> gives
<CODE>a</CODE> if it counts as true, and otherwise <CODE>b</CODE>. So <CODE>c.last || c.first ||
c.company || '(no name)'</CODE> gives the first of them that isn't empty.<P>
<CODE>compare</CODE> is a compare function for <CODE>sort</CODE>, as in chapter 5 of the first book. It
compares surnames (or companies) first, then first names, and if both are the same, the ids, so the order is
always the same. Instead of <CODE>&lt;</CODE> it uses <CODE>localeCompare</CODE>, a string method that
compares the way a dictionary does: <CODE>&lt;</CODE> puts every capital letter before every small one, so
"de Vere" would come after "Young", but <CODE>localeCompare</CODE> puts it among the Ds. With
<CODE>{ sensitivity: 'base' }</CODE> it also counts a capital and a small letter, or a letter with an accent
and one without, as the same, so "Smith" and "smith" are equal.<P>
<CODE>compare</CODE> isn't exported: it's the Book's own business how it sorts.<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>The change event</H2>
Every time <CODE>add</CODE> adds a contact, it calls <CODE>this.emit('change', ...)</CODE>. That's what a Book
gets from being an Emitter. Emitter is the first module of the toolkit:<P>
<PRE>
export class Emitter {
  constructor() {
    this.listeners = new Map();      // event name -&gt; Set of functions
  }

  /** Call fn(data) whenever the event happens; returns off(). */
  on(type, fn) {
    if (!this.listeners.has(type)) {
      this.listeners.set(type, new Set());
    }
    this.listeners.get(type).add(fn);
    return () =&gt; this.listeners.get(type).delete(fn);
  }

  /** Tell every listener that the event has happened. */
  emit(type, data) {
    for (const fn of [...(this.listeners.get(type) ?? [])]) fn(data);
  }
}
</PRE>
An Emitter keeps a list of functions to call for each kind of event. <CODE>on('change', fn)</CODE> adds a
function to the list for 'change'; <CODE>emit('change', data)</CODE> calls every function on it. So any part of
the program can ask to be told when the Book changes, and the Book doesn't need to know who's asking.<P>
Two details:<P>
<UL>
<LI>The listeners for each event are kept in a <I>Set</I>, which is like an array that can't hold the same
thing twice, and can remove things quickly: <CODE>add</CODE>, <CODE>has</CODE> and <CODE>delete</CODE> work
as they do for Maps.<P>
<LI><CODE>on</CODE> returns a function. Calling it removes the listener again - useful when a window that
was listening is closed.<P>
</UL>
Nothing listens to the Book in this chapter. In chapter 4 the list in the window will listen, so it can
redraw itself whenever the Book changes, whoever changed it.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Testing the model</H2>
Because the model has nothing to do with windows, you can test it with a simple program that makes a Book,
does things to it and checks the answers. That's <B>Check</B>. Double-click it (it's a module, so it runs like
the first book's modules) and it prints what it tested:<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C1Check" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
Here is the part that does the testing:<P>
<PRE>
function check(what, got, want) {
  const ok = same(got, want);
  if (!ok) failed++;
  print(`${ok ? 'ok  ' : 'FAIL'} ${what}`);
  if (!ok) print(`     got ${JSON.stringify(got)}`);
}

export default function start() {
  // ------------------------------------------------ the Book
  const book = new Book();
  let changes = 0;
  book.on('change', () =&gt; changes++);
  const ann = book.add({ first: 'Ann', last: 'Smith' });
  const bob = book.add({ first: 'Bob', last: 'Jones',
    company: 'Acorn' });
  book.add({ company: 'Zebra Ltd' });
  check('add gives ids', [ann.id, bob.id], [1, 2]);
  check('changes are told', changes, 3);
  check('sorted by surname', book.list().map(nameOf),
    ['Jones, Bob', 'Smith, Ann', 'Zebra Ltd']);

  print(failed ? `${failed} FAILED` : 'all ok');
}
</PRE>
<CODE>check(what, got, want)</CODE> prints <CODE>ok</CODE> if <CODE>got</CODE> is the same as
<CODE>want</CODE>, and <CODE>FAIL</CODE> and what it got if not. (<CODE>same</CODE>, above it, compares two
values by turning them into JSON text; the lines before it make sure an object's properties are compared in
the same order.)<P>
Two lines use the <I>conditional operator</I>, <CODE>a ? b : c</CODE>: it's a short <CODE>if</CODE> that gives a
value - <CODE>b</CODE> if <CODE>a</CODE> counts as true, otherwise <CODE>c</CODE>. So
<CODE>ok ? 'ok  ' : 'FAIL'</CODE> is <CODE>'ok  '</CODE> when the test passed, and the last line prints
<I>all ok</I> only if <CODE>failed</CODE> is 0 (which counts as false). The book uses it for small choices like
these; for anything longer, <CODE>if</CODE> is clearer.<P>
Testing like this is worth the few minutes it takes. Every time you change the model, double-click Check: if
it still says <I>all ok</I>, you haven't broken anything it tests. Each chapter adds tests for what it adds,
and the finished !Contacts' Check tests fifteen things.<P>
<B>Note</B>: try breaking the model on purpose - change the <CODE>compare</CODE> function to sort by first
name - and run Check again. The test called <I>sorted by surname</I> fails, and shows what it got.<P>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>A first window</H2>
Finally, something to look at. MainWindow shows the contacts' names and telephone numbers:<P>
<PRE>
// MainWindow - the window showing an address book. For now it just
// lists the contacts' names and telephone numbers; closing it ends
// the program.
import { wimpColour } from 'riscos';
import { nameOf, phoneOf } from './Model';

const ROW = 20;                      // the height of a line

export class MainWindow {
  constructor(task, book) {
    this.book = book;
    const w = this.win = task.createWindow({
      title: 'Contacts', x: 120, y: 70,
      w: 600, h: 380, extent: { w: 600, h: 380 },
      colours: { workBg: 0 },
      flags: { back: true, close: true, title: true, toggle: true,
        vscroll: true, size: true, moveable: true },
    });
    w.useCanvas((g) =&gt; this.draw(g));
    w.on('close', () =&gt; task.quit());
    w.open({ behind: 'top' });
  }

  /** Draw a line for each contact: its name, then its phone. */
  draw(g) {
    g.font = '14px Homerton';
    g.textBaseline = 'middle';
    g.fillStyle = wimpColour(7);
    this.book.list().forEach((c, i) =&gt; {
      const y = 8 + i * ROW + ROW / 2;
      g.fillText(nameOf(c), 8, y);
      g.fillText(phoneOf(c), 220, y);
    });
  }
}
</PRE>
MainWindow is a class too: <CODE>new MainWindow(task, book)</CODE> in !RunImage makes one, and its constructor
makes the window. It keeps the Book in <CODE>this.book</CODE> and the window in <CODE>this.win</CODE> (the
line <CODE>const w = this.win = ...</CODE> sets both at once), so its <CODE>draw</CODE> method can use them
later. The drawing is done as in the first book, one line of text for each contact in
<CODE>this.book.list()</CODE>. <CODE>forEach((c, i) =&gt; ...)</CODE> calls the arrow function for each contact
in turn; the second thing it's given, <CODE>i</CODE>, is the contact's position in the list (0, 1, 2 ...), which
works out how far down to draw it.<P>
Double-click !Contacts1 to run it:<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C1List" WIDTH=629 HEIGHT=409></CENTER>
It isn't much yet: you can't choose a contact, change one or add one. But it has the shape of the finished
application - a model, a view, and a program to test the model - and every chapter from here on adds to that
shape rather than changing it.<P>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Plan an application by writing down what it remembers and what happens.<P>
<LI>Keep the <I>model</I> (the information and its rules) apart from the <I>view</I> (the windows).<P>
<LI>A <CODE>class</CODE> describes a kind of object; <CODE>new</CODE> makes one; the
<CODE>constructor</CODE> sets it up; <CODE>this</CODE> is the object; its functions are methods;
<CODE>get</CODE> makes a method you use like a property.<P>
<LI><CODE>extends</CODE> makes a class a special kind of another; call <CODE>super()</CODE> first in its
constructor.<P>
<LI>A <CODE>Map</CODE> finds a value by its key quickly; a <CODE>Set</CODE> holds things without repeats.<P>
<LI><CODE>a ?? b</CODE> gives <CODE>b</CODE> only if <CODE>a</CODE> is null or undefined; <CODE>a || b</CODE>
gives <CODE>b</CODE> if <CODE>a</CODE> counts as false; <CODE>a ? b : c</CODE> gives <CODE>b</CODE> or
<CODE>c</CODE>; <CODE>{ ...obj }</CODE> and <CODE>[...list]</CODE> copy.<P>
<LI>An Emitter lets other parts of a program listen for events with <CODE>on</CODE>; <CODE>emit</CODE>
tells them.<P>
<LI>Test the model with a program of its own, and run it after every change.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add a third column showing each contact's company.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add a test to Check that a contact added with an id of its own (say
<CODE>{ id: 20, first: 'Zoe' }</CODE>) keeps it, and that the next new contact gets 21.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Add a method <CODE>inCategory(name)</CODE> to the Book that gives the contacts in one category,
sorted, and a test for it.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Change the window so that contacts with no telephone number show <I>(none)</I> in grey.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  In <CODE>draw</CODE>, after the phone (<CODE>?? ''</CODE> because a contact may have no
company):
<PRE>
g.fillText(c.company ?? '', 380, y);
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  In Check, after the other tests:
<PRE>
const zoe = book.add({ id: 20, first: 'Zoe' });
check('own id kept', zoe.id, 20);
check('next id', book.add({ first: 'Yan' }).id, 21);
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  In the Book class, after <CODE>list</CODE>:
<PRE>
inCategory(name) {
  return this.list().filter((c) =&gt; c.category === name);
}
</PRE>
and in Check (none of Check's contacts has a category yet, so add one that has):
<PRE>
const b = book.add({ first: 'Bea', last: 'Lee', category: 'Work' });
check('in category', book.inCategory('Work'), [b]);
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  In <CODE>draw</CODE>, work out the phone first and choose the colour:
<PRE>
const phone = phoneOf(c);
if (phone) g.fillText(phone, 220, y);
else {
  g.fillStyle = wimpColour(4);
  g.fillText('(none)', 220, y);
  g.fillStyle = wimpColour(7);
}
</PRE>
</DL>

